// Machine-kind content (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.6) — data, never hardcoded in
// sim_core. This is the `machines.ron` CLAUDE.md's Project Structure section
// has been anticipating ("items.ron/machines.ron/contracts.ron land here too
// when they exist"); the "Machines improvement" milestone's ticket 1 is what
// finally adds it.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader (`sim_core::sim::machines::MachineRegistry`) rejects a
// mismatched version rather than silently misreading a changed shape, the
// same way `recipes.ron`'s loader does.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. Every `Recipe.machine` string in
// `data/recipes.ron` names a kind defined here, and the loader errors
// clearly if it names one that isn't (see `MachineRegistry::validate_covers`
// — a typo'd kind is caught at construction time, not as "a stage silently
// never renders"). Right now a kind carries exactly one property, its tile
// footprint, because that is what `World`'s chain layout needs in order to
// stop assuming every machine is one tile wide. Per-kind staff/power draw
// (GAMEPLAY.md §7), build cost (GAMEPLAY.md §4/§5), and required-equipment
// tier all belong in this file too when those systems land — add fields
// here, don't invent a second machine data file.
//
// FOOTPRINT, AND WHERE ITS ORIGIN IS. `footprint` is measured in world
// tiles: `width_tiles` along +X (the direction the production chain runs),
// `height_tiles` along +Y. A machine occupies the rectangle
// `x in [origin.x, origin.x + width_tiles)`, `y in [origin.y, origin.y +
// height_tiles)` — i.e. **the origin tile is the footprint's minimum-X,
// minimum-Y corner**. (A belt used to have to run west to east into that
// corner; since the "Real Placement" epic a machine is fed on every tile of
// the edge opposite its facing, from a belt at any facing, and the belt is
// drawn connected to it whichever way both point — the pipes and conveyors
// overhaul retired the east-only belt junction art.) See
// `sim_core/src/sim/machines.rs`'s module doc for the full statement of the
// origin convention; it is what lets
// presentation place a multi-tile machine unambiguously from a single
// origin tile plus these two numbers.
//
// SIZES ARE A DELIBERATE CONTENT CHOICE (ticket 2). Ticket 1 shipped every
// kind as 1x1 so that generalizing the layout math moved no tile at all;
// ticket 2 is the content change that ticket 1 existed to enable. The chain
// front end is now three thematically real machines — a crusher, a mixer and
// a cooking vat — and **the cooking vat is 2x2**, the first kind in the game
// that is bigger than one tile. Size here is meant to read as "this machine
// is a serious piece of equipment," so a kind only grows when its real-world
// counterpart genuinely is bulky: a bench-scale crusher or mixer is not, a
// cooking vat that holds a batch of sauce for a long cook very much is. See
// each kind's own note below.
//
// EVERY KIND HERE IS PLACED BY THE PLAYER (the "Real Placement" epic). A
// footprint used to describe where `World` would *build* something; it now
// describes what a player claims when they put one down, and it rotates:
// facing north or south swaps `width_tiles` and `height_tiles` on the grid,
// anchored at the same origin corner. See `sim_core/src/sim/machines.rs`'s
// module doc for the full rotation convention. The `mixer` below is the
// first shipped kind that is genuinely oblong (1x2), so it is also the first
// whose footprint really does change shape when turned — which is worth
// knowing before rotating one against a wall.
//
// A FOOTPRINT'S INLET FACE IS AS LONG AS THE FOOTPRINT (the chain-rework
// epic). A machine accepts solid input on every tile of the edge opposite
// the way it points, so **a machine that consumes N different solid
// ingredients needs an inlet face at least N tiles long** — otherwise its
// one inlet tile is a merge point, and a merge point with no splitter or
// filtered insertion behind it deadlocks: whichever ingredient arrives
// faster fills the shared belt and starves the other one out. That is the
// concrete reason the `mixer` grew to 1x2 when its recipe gained sugar, and
// it is a rule any future multi-ingredient kind has to satisfy too.
//
// A kind also has to be priced in `data/shop.ron` to be placeable at all, and
// the loader enforces that: adding a kind here without a price there is an
// error, not a thing the player can never build.
//
// FOUR KINDS HERE ARE NOT A RECIPE'S MACHINE (ticket 3, the "Visual
// overhaul round 2" epic's ticket 1, the delivery-dock ticket, and Epic B's
// splitter). Every other kind in this file is named by some `Recipe.machine`
// in `data/recipes.ron`; the `hopper`, the `fluid_tank`, the `dock` and the
// `splitter` are not. Each moves product without turning anything into
// anything — the hopper holds raw tomatoes and meters them onto the first
// belt, the fluid tank holds vinegar and feeds it into the pipe run, the
// dock takes finished boxes off the last belt and ships them into the
// economy's shared delivery pool, the splitter takes one belt's product and
// deals it out to two — so none of them has a recipe, and all four are
// built as their own body types (`sim_core/src/sim/hopper.rs`,
// `sim_core/src/sim/fluid.rs`, `sim_core/src/sim/dock.rs`,
// `sim_core/src/sim/splitter.rs`) rather than as a `Machine`. They still
// live in this file because a footprint is a footprint: they are placed
// buildings that claim tiles, and nothing else should have to know a second
// way of answering "how big is that thing."
//
// ORDER IN THIS FILE IS NOT THE CHAIN, and since the "Real Placement" epic
// nothing else is either: there is no chain in the simulation at all, only
// placements and the adjacency between them. `data/recipes.ron`'s order
// still describes one production line — it is what `sim::reference_chain`
// (the test fixture that rebuilds the old shipped layout out of purchase and
// placement commands) lays out, and what the raw-ingredient rule reads — but
// `World` no longer builds anything from it.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    machines: [
        // The chain's raw-ingredient feeder (ticket 3) — one of the three
        // kinds here with no recipe behind it; see the note above. 1x1: a
        // feed hopper sitting over the head of a single belt is a bin, not a
        // hall, and the whole point of it is that its *buffer* is generous,
        // not its floor space. Where it stands is now the player's decision;
        // it discharges onto whatever belt is off the face it points at.
        MachineKind(
            id: "hopper",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // The pipe run's supply (the "Visual overhaul round 2" epic's
        // ticket 1) — the fluid twin of the hopper above, and another
        // kind here with no recipe behind it. It replaced milestone 6's
        // always-refilling off-map tap, so the vinegar the mixer needs is
        // now something the player loads and can run out of.
        //
        // 1x1, for the hopper's reason and one of its own. A single
        // stainless vessel standing at the head of a four-tile pipe run is a
        // drum, not a tank farm, and — like the hopper — what is meant to be
        // generous about it is its *buffer* (120 units, see
        // `FLUID_TANK_CAPACITY`), not its floor space. It discharges into
        // whichever pipe tile is off the face it points at, wherever the
        // player stands it.
        MachineKind(
            id: "fluid_tank",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // A conveyor splitter (Epic B) — one belt in, two belts out, and
        // the FOURTH kind in this file with no recipe behind it. Like the
        // hopper, the tank and the dock it moves product without changing
        // it, so it is a placed building with a footprint rather than a
        // `Recipe.machine`; unlike those three it is *belt-like*, holding
        // what it carries in an ordinary one-tile `BeltSegment` (see
        // `sim_core/src/sim/splitter.rs`).
        //
        // 1x1, and this one is not a judgement call: a splitter has to be
        // interchangeable with the belt tile it stands in place of, or it
        // could not be dropped into an existing line. It is the same
        // rotation convention as everything else here with one addition
        // that is stated in `splitter.rs` rather than invented per call
        // site — the placement's `direction` is the straight-through
        // output, and the second output is one quarter turn CLOCKWISE from
        // it, so rotating the building turns both outputs together and no
        // second facing field exists to drift out of step with the first.
        MachineKind(
            id: "splitter",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // GAMEPLAY.md §2 tier 1's raw-ingredient prep: crushes whole
        // tomatoes into paste. 1x1 — a crusher is a hopper and a screw, the
        // physically smallest machine on the line, and it is deliberately
        // NOT the milestone's "big machine" example.
        MachineKind(
            id: "crusher",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // Blends tomato paste and sugar with vinegar into an uncooked
        // ketchup mix, and discharges that mix as a **liquid** into a second
        // pipe run. It is therefore the first kind here plumbed on both
        // sides at once — it draws from a network touching its footprint and
        // deposits into the one off its outlet face — so its footprint
        // decides where two runs can reach it, not one.
        //
        // **1x2, and the only oblong kind in this file.** One tile wide,
        // because an in-line blender is a small unit; two tiles *deep*
        // because its recipe takes two different solid ingredients (tomato
        // paste and sugar) and, per the header note above, each one needs
        // its own tile of inlet face to arrive on. A 1x1 mixer would force
        // both onto one belt, and that merge deadlocks as soon as either
        // ingredient outruns the other. The extra tile is not decoration:
        // it is the second feed chute, and it is what makes a two-ingredient
        // recipe buildable at all today.
        MachineKind(
            id: "mixer",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 2),
        ),
        // GAMEPLAY.md §2 tier 1/2's cooker/kettle, grown up: a batch vat
        // that cooks the mix down into finished ketchup. **This is the
        // milestone's big machine, and the only kind that is not 1x1.**
        //
        // Since the chain rework it is also the one machine on the line
        // with **no belt attached to it at all**: liquid mix arrives by
        // pipe and finished ketchup leaves by pipe, which is what a batch
        // cook in a real plant looks like.
        //
        // 2x2, not 2x1, for two reasons. Thematically, a vat is the one
        // machine on this line whose bulk is the point — it holds a whole
        // batch through the chain's longest process (240 ticks, three times
        // the crush), so "it takes up real floor space and it is worth it"
        // is exactly the read we want. Mechanically, 2x2 is the smallest
        // footprint that exercises ticket 1's mechanism in *both* axes:
        // width pushes every downstream stage east by one extra tile, and
        // height means the machine claims a second row, which a
        // width-only 2x1 would never test outside a synthetic fixture.
        // Bigger than that (a 3x3, say) would dwarf the belts feeding it
        // and start to look absurd rather than substantial.
        MachineKind(
            id: "cooking_vat",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 2, height_tiles: 2),
        ),
        // Tier 2's bottling & sealing line — and, since the chain rework,
        // the stage where the product stops being a fluid: it draws liquid
        // ketchup out of a pipe run touching its footprint and puts bottles
        // onto the belt off its outlet face.
        MachineKind(
            id: "bottler",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // Packing finished bottles into boxes — the stage milestone 4 added
        // as its proof that extending the chain is a data-file-only edit.
        MachineKind(
            id: "packer",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 1, height_tiles: 1),
        ),
        // The chain's finished-product exit (the delivery-dock ticket) — the
        // third kind here with no recipe behind it, and the exact mirror of
        // the `hopper` above. Boxes leave the packer onto one more ordinary
        // belt, that belt runs into this building, and the dock deposits
        // what arrives into the economy's shared delivery pool every tick,
        // with no player command involved. It replaced
        // `PlayerCommand::CollectOutput`, which used to reach into the last
        // machine's own output buffer from nowhere.
        //
        // **2x2 since the 2026-09-09 tuning pass** — the second kind in this
        // file bigger than one tile, and the only one that grew because of
        // how it READS rather than because of what it does.
        //
        // It was 1x1 on the argument that "a loading bay at the end of one
        // belt is a bay, not a warehouse, and what is meant to be generous
        // about it is its *buffer* (32 units, see `DOCK_CAPACITY`), not its
        // floor space." Everything in that sentence about the buffer still
        // holds — the buffer is a pass-through anyway, since the dock ships
        // everything it receives in the same tick — but the buffer was the
        // wrong thing to size the FOOTPRINT by, and a playtest said so: at
        // one tile the dock did not read as a loading dock. It is the one
        // building on the line that is architecture rather than equipment
        // (UI_UX.md §2b point 16), and everything that makes it legible as
        // one — a raised deck a trailer backs up to, a striped edge, rubber
        // bumpers, a despatch door under a canopy, and clear apron for the
        // goods waiting to go out — was competing for a single 32-voxel
        // tile. That crowding is exactly why the stack of waiting cases on
        // its deck had to be capped at two: a fourth case buried the
        // building it was standing on.
        //
        // **2x2 rather than 2x1, and DEPTH is the reason.** UI_UX.md §2b
        // point 16's rule for this camera is "put the height at high y, put
        // the content at low y": anything tall has to stand on the FAR row
        // or it walls off everything behind it. A 2x1 dock is twice the
        // frontage and still exactly one row deep, so the despatch
        // structure and the goods standing on the deck would go on fighting
        // for the same row — it would buy width the layout does not need
        // and none of the depth it does. The second row is the whole point.
        //
        // Two things fall out of the shape, and neither is decoration.
        // (1) Its inlet face is now **two tiles long** (see the header rule
        // above), so two belt lines can deliver into one dock instead of
        // having to merge upstream of it — the same property that made the
        // mixer 1x2, arriving at the other end of the line. (2) 2x2 is
        // **square**, so a rotated dock claims exactly the same four tiles
        // whichever way it faces. That matters most for the building at the
        // END of a line: a player turning it to meet a belt arriving from
        // another direction should not discover that the tile behind it is
        // no longer free, which is precisely what an oblong terminal
        // building would do.
        //
        // Its price in `data/shop.ron` deliberately did NOT move with the
        // footprint — see that file's own dock entry for why.
        //
        // Unlike the hopper and the tank it is placed EAST of the chain
        // rather than west, because it is the tail rather than the head. A
        // footprint straddling one of ARCHITECTURE.md §3.1's 32x32 chunk
        // boundaries is handled rather than avoided — `World::all_chunks`
        // reports every chunk anything stands in, and a footprint is
        // reported in every chunk it overlaps — which now applies to this
        // kind itself, not only to kinds upstream of it: a two-tile-wide
        // dock is one of the things that can land astride x = 32.
        MachineKind(
            id: "dock",
            footprint: Footprint(width_tiles: 2, height_tiles: 2),
        ),
    ],
)
